  has been going on in russia during the  donbass conflict so for eight years  they've been documenting these cases  it's very difficult to bring them to  justice as well uh the prosecutor you  know is is very adamant that they are  going to ensure that these these cases  be brought to justice um they're setting  up what uh like a war crimes tribunal  similar to the ones post rwanda and post  bosnia and they hope that they will be  able to you know bring from the highest  levels down i mean people wanting to  even bring you know make sure that  president putin pays for these crimes  but as the ic um russia is not signed up  to the icc as we know so it will be  difficult but you know even president  zolensky himself saying he's determined  to make sure that that people get tried  for these crimes dw's rebecca ritters  thank you  catalina busole is a ukrainian lawyer  specializing in international  humanitarian and criminal law and she  joins us now from london thanks for  being with us katrina how likely is it  that the perpetrators of prey of rape in  the ukraine war will ever be brought to  justice  um good afternoon and um i must say that  the attention of the international  community and actually of domestic  investigators and prosecutors in ukraine  towards sexual violence has been  unprecedented since february 2022  but it's important to stress that of  course russia's actions russia's  aggression towards ukraine started much  early in 2014 and during that period  although there were quite numerous  reports about rapes and other forms of  sexual violence such as sexualized  torture or threats of rapes to a  survivor or to his or her family member  there were not enough domestic  prosecutions there was only one  communication to the international  criminal court prepared and there were  no  so far as we know prosecutions at the  domestic levels in other countries in  foreign state under the principle of  universal jurisdiction now with these  new accounts horrific accounts of rape  and other forms of sexual violence post  february 2022 we see that this  particular alleged war crime potentially  a crime against humanity and the  potential  crime of genocide as mentioned by ombuds  woman denisova receives far more  attention so at this point it's crucial  to harmonize all the documentation  efforts to ensure that they're  sensitized and that they don't  re-traumatize the survivors because  there's already a problem that several  survivors are being  interviewed by human rights lawyers  investigators and prosecutors for  several times which of course caused the  additional trauma for them and then of  course to see how these cases proceed  not only to the ukrainian courts and the  prosecutor general seems to pay  particular attention to sexual violence  now but also to foreign jurisdictions  where many survivors end up as refugees  and also to the international criminal  courts  gathering testimony and evidence in such  sensitive cases is difficult what do you  see as the main challenges in bringing  these cases to prosecution  as i mentioned there are two challenges  to harmonize the documentation efforts  at first because so many actors are  involved now in the conflict that has  been ongoing for eight years but the  activation of efforts domestic and  especially international has happened  only post february 2022 so there should  be more of coordination between the  numerous human rights ngos documentation  initiatives uh their harmonization of  their efforts with those of ukrainian  investigators and prosecutors and of  course also there should be more  alignment in terms of what focus will uh  the foreign teams that consult ukrainian  prosecutor office of the prosecutor  general and the investigative team of  the icc uh what they will have uh and  this is important to ensure in  particular with such  sensitive group of survivors survivors  of sexual violence and pre-february this  year ukraine uh to my knowledge and to  